  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain showers and thunderstorms this afternoon. \nThere is a marginal risk for severe thunderstorms this afternoon\nand evening. Locally heavy rainfall is possible which could \nlead to minor urban flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system approaches from the west today. Frontal \npassage is anticipated early this morning as a warm front lifts\nover the area, leaving northern and eastern Maine in the warm \nsector of the system. Ample moisture moves in along frontal \nboundary, with PWATs in the 1-2 inch range. Generally looking \nat higher rainfall totals along the Downeast coast, with the \ncoast receiving around 1  1.4 inches. Roughly 0.5  1 inches in\nCentral Highlands and northern Maine. \n\nThere is a chance for some thunderstorms to move through in the\nnorth and Central Highlands/Upper Penobscot Valley regions, as \nthe associated cold front with the system moves through this \nafternoon into evening. CAMs are picking up on some CAPE, DCAPE,\ndecent shear parameters, low-level lapse rates, and low CIN. \nSkies will be mostly overcast throughout the area, however,\nwhich could cause a damper in convection. North Woods and \nAroostook region seems to have the best potential for \nconvection, especially if there is any bit of clearing in the \ncloud cover. That being said, instability does exist throughout \nnorthern and eastern Maine, and any location with some sky \nclearing and along the front (which will move from west to east \nduring the afternoon), could see some developing storms. There \nis also a potential for some, albeit light, Canadian wildfire \nsmoke to move in along thew cold front boundary, which would \nalso cause less convection. Greatest threats for these storms \nwill be lightning, heavy rain, and gusty winds. Heavy rainfall \ncould result in localized urban/poor-drainage flooding.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous rip currents and high surf along the \nDowneast coast today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nUnusually rough seas by late July standards are expected to \npeak today. This is usually the quietest time of year on the \nwaters, but seas in the neighborhood of 6-9 feet are expected up\nto the coastline, with a 9-10 second period swell from the \nsouth. People along the coast should exercise caution if \nrecreating. Water temperatures in the 50s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Potential for a system moving in early next \nweek, bringing another round of wetting rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nA ridge of high pressure will crest over the forecast area this\nweekend, and during this time temperatures could build to\nslightly above seasonal averages with highs in the low to mid\n80s. By Monday next week, another shortwave may approach from\nthe west, bringing the next round of moisture into the area that\ncould charge the atmosphere for more rainfall across the CWA.\nRecent guidance suggests the bulk of the instability with this\nsystem will hold off until Tuesday at the earliest, where more\nCAPE could lead to the development of some thunderstorms. More\nmoisture on Tuesday may also support more efficient rain\nprocesses.",
